] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7eb8a72b-0beb-5d49-b077-ac32cdeca74e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362354 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0965ed17-df89-5de8-b0cd-60d291248cdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363465 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363466 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1ca7b7a8-4b09-595e-a90a-557478166188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363467 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363467 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363467 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92fa23c5-f5ca-52b3-b9c6-f9d5a4e10d47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363468 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{2}-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363468 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363468 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "96364a64-4346